    {"old": "- **The Bash-hunk recipe can intermittently record an empty diff** (observed ~1 in 10 on fast runs): `worktreeTree` diffs two `git write-tree` snapshots taken on a *copied* index (`jev-rule-blocks/index-<pid>`), and the copy's fresh mtime clears git's racily-clean guard, so a same-size rewrite landing in the same wall-clock second as the index's recorded stat is trusted as unchanged. Symptom: snapshot consumed, `files: []`, `hunks: []`, `partial: false`, no error row. Retry — or write different-length content — before calling it a failure.",
     "new": "- **Same-second same-size rewrites are the racy edge.** `worktreeTree` diffs two `git write-tree` snapshots taken on a copied index (`jev-rule-blocks/index-<pid>`); git only rehashes a file whose stat mismatches the index entry or whose entry is racy (recorded mtime not older than the index write). The copy now preserves the source index's mtime (`utimesSync`, 0.29.1) so the scratch index makes the same racy call — before that fix the copy's fresh mtime cleared the guard and ~1 in 10 fast-loop writes silently recorded an empty diff (snapshot consumed, `files: []`, `partial: false`, no error). If an empty diff shows up again on current code, it is a new bug, not this flake."}
